To find critical value for F statistic, you need these information

alpha <- 0.05 (for 95% significance level)
df1 <- 2 (the number of restrictions in your hypothesis-the numerator)
df2 <- 495 (n-k where k is the number of parameters- the denomenator)

Fcrit <- qf(alpha,df1,df2,lower.tail = FALSE) <-- ensure lower tail is FALSE otherwise the default code is TRUE
Ans: 3.01

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------

To compute the test statistic, you need these information which can be found in R with the anova() function.

SSR_r <- sum of squares (restricted)
SSR_u <- sum of squares (unrestricted)
r <- number of restrictions
n <- sample size
k <- number of permutations including beta_o the null hypothesis

t_test <- (SSR_r - SSR_u / r) / (SSR_u * (n-k))

#------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Do you accept or reject the null hypothesis at 95% confidence interval?

Ans: You should reject large values of the test statistic.
As calculated above, if Fcrit < t_test, then you should reject.
